\paragraph{Out-of-distribution evaluation.}
To test whether the PPL improvement reflects general quality or distribution matching, we evaluate on two corpora outside FineWeb (seed\,42; checkpoints for other seeds not retained).
Adaptive reweighting shows higher PPL on OpenWebText ($\Delta{=}{+}0.06$) and Wikipedia ($\Delta{=}{+}0.14$), while improving on FineWeb holdout ($\Delta{=}{-}0.04$), indicating distribution matching rather than general quality gain.

tool_args.old_string:
\paragraph{Why minimum intervention works.}
The $\aeff$ quality constraint prevents 4.9$\times$ over-aggressive downweighting in 38.2\% of batches (\S\ref{sec:regime}), robust across contamination levels (33.4\% at $\rho{=}0.6$) and seeds (CV ${<}2\%$).
Using the aggregate mean $\bstar \approx 1.4$ as a fixed exponent yields PPL comparable to uniform (17.693 vs.\ 17.694), confirming per-batch adaptation is essential.

